  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe forecast remains strongly tied to radar and satellite trends\nheading into the end of the work week. A frontal boundary draped\nthrough the heart of the Ohio Valley continues to serve up\nthunderstorm complexes with a slew of damaging winds and\ntorrential rainfall. The Central Appalachians has lucked out the\npast few days as the curvature in the boundary has caused \nstorms to travel mostly through Kentucky southward, and only \njust skirting our western counties. That may not be the case \nheading into tonight and Friday, as the orientation of the front\nis progged to lift back to the north, planting the forecast \narea back into the corridor of shower and storm coverage. \n\nA wide array of model solutions for timing and magnitude of\nstorms remains at play on Friday, with some driving storms into\nthe area as early as the pre-dawn hours on Friday, while others\nmaintain an active afternoon and evening period. Despite the\nuncertainty, enough of a signal is present in the near term\nforecast period for increased potential of localized flash\nflooding from heavy downpours beyond the current Flash Flood\nWatch timeframe. Opted to therefore extend the Watch into Friday\nevening to encapsulate more of the potential daytime activity\nmeandering over the area. Anomalously high PWATs that have been\naccompanying storms this week could exacerbate already saturated\nsoils across our basins, despite having had a day or two to \nrecover from previous rainfall. Additional updates to the Watch\nmay be warranted following future model guidance cycles and \nmonitoring upstream radar trends.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe weekend forecast continues to teeter towards a more active\nstate than what previous model guidance has conveyed. While\nstrong high pressure in the Upper Midwest forces our stalled\nboundary to shift into New England, diurnally driven showers and\nstorms remains feasible Saturday afternoon along the higher\nterrain and across our northern counties where the front may\nstill fester. A cold front arriving from the west brings\nadditional precipitation chances for the end of the weekend, and\ncould also impose active weather into next week in the event the\nboundary gets hung up in close proximity to the forecast area.\nTemperatures through the period will likely stay above normal, \nexcept a brief drop to near normal this weekend with the frontal\npassage.",
